Default network protocol and network feature timers.

! Spanning Tree
 - BPDU Timer
   - max-age:  20 sec
   - hello:  2 sec

 - BPDU Type 2 Timer ( RSTP )
   - hello:  2 sec
   - TC-While timer:  2 x hello timer ( 4 sec )

 - BPDU Type 3 Timer ( MST )
   - hello:  2 sec

 - STP Port States
   - Blocking:  20 sec
   - Listening:  15 sec
   - Learning:  15 sec
   - When a port first comes up:
     - 50 sec ( 20 + 15 + 15 )


! CAM Table
 - aging:  300 sec
 - BPDU with TC bit set:  forwarding time ( 15 sec )


! GRE Tunnel
 - keepalive:  10 sec


! BGP timers
 - advertisement
   - determines how quickly route updates propogate
   - eBGP sessions not in a VRF:  30 sec
   - eBGP sessions in a VRF:  0 sec
   - iBGP sessions:  0 sec
 - keepalive:  60 sec
   - determines how quickly software failures are detected
 - hold time:  180 sec
   - determines how quickly software failures are detected
 - retry time:  120 sec
   - determines how quickly a failed connection is retried
 - scan time:  60 sec
   - the BGP scanner
   - validates next-hop addresses are still reachable
   - checks conditional-advertisements
   - imports routes into a VRF
   - exports routes from a VRF
   - the BGP Event-Based VPN Import feature is event driven, when a BGP path changes
     - applies when a path is imported from a BGP VPN table into a BGP VRF table
     - configuration changes, such as adding imported route-targets to a VRF are still handled by the BGP scanner
 - trigger delay:  5 sec
   - BGP next-hop tracking
 - update delay:  120 sec
   - time from when a neighbor is established until the best path algorithm is run and updates are sent


! RIP
 - update timer:  30 sec
 - invalid timer:  180 sec
 - holddown timer:  180 sec
 - flush timer:  240 sec


! EIGRP
 - hello:
   - T1 ( or slower ):  60 sec
   - all other network types:  5 sec
 - hold-time:
   - low-speed NBMA networks:  180 sec
   - all other network types:  15 sec


! OSPF
 - Hello Interval
   - Broadcast networks:  10 sec
   - Non-broadcast networks:  30 sec
   - Point-to-point networks:  10 sec
 - Dead Timer
   - 4 x the hello interval
 - Wait Timer
   - Wait period to select a DR
   - 4 x the hello interval
 - Fast Hello Packets
   - Intervals less than 1 sec
   - ip ospf dead-interval min hello-multiplier 5
   - ip ospf hello-interval 1


! OSPF Network Types
 - Broadcast
   - hello:  10 sec
   - dead-interval:  40 sec
 - Non-broadcast
   - hello:  30 sec
   - dead-interval:  120 sec
 - Point-to-point
   - hello:  10 sec
   - dead-interval:  40 sec
 - Point-to-multipoint
   - hello:  30 sec
   - dead-interval:  120 sec
 - Point-to-multipoint non-broadcast
   - hello:  30 sec
   - dead-interval:  120 sec


